(This report presents a complete laboratory experiment on automated weed detection using) Tj
T*
(a Random Forest) Tj
T*
(classifier. The system is developed using a synthetic, domain-inspired dataset that) Tj
T*
(models agriculturally) Tj
T*
(meaningful features: GreenIndex, Texture, Moisture, Height, and NDVI. A total of 1000) Tj
T*
(samples were generated,) Tj
T*
(with 601 crop samples and 399 weed samples, to simulate a moderately imbalanced field) Tj
T*
(setting. The model was) Tj
T*
(trained using a stratified 80:20 train-test split, and hyperparameters were tuned using) Tj
T*
(GridSearchCV with) Tj
T*
(5-fold StratifiedKFold cross-validation.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The best Random Forest configuration achieved a cross-validated F1-score of 0.929. On) Tj
T*
(the held-out test set,) Tj
T*
(the tuned model achieved 94.0% accuracy, 94.7% precision, 90.0% recall, and a 92.3%) Tj
T*
(F1-score. The confusion) Tj
T*
(matrix showed strong overall performance, with 116 out of 120 crop samples and 72 out of) Tj
T*
(80 weed samples) Tj
T*
(correctly classified. Feature-importance analysis identified NDVI as the strongest) Tj
T*
(predictor, followed by) Tj
T*
(GreenIndex and Height. These results show that Random Forest provides a strong,) Tj
T*
(interpretable baseline for weed) Tj
T*
(detection in a laboratory setting and can support future work using real crop imagery or) Tj
T*
(sensor measurements.) Tj
