(6. Methodology) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(6.1 Data Generation and Preparation) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(A synthetic dataset was generated using domain-inspired distributions for crop and weed) Tj
T*
(samples. Crop samples) Tj
T*
(were assigned generally higher GreenIndex, NDVI, and Height values, while weed samples) Tj
T*
(showed different) Tj
T*
(distributions in texture, moisture, and spectral response. A small amount of label noise) Tj
T*
(was added to avoid an) Tj
T*
(unrealistically perfect separation between the two classes.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The dataset was divided using a stratified 80:20 train-test split with random_state =) Tj
T*
(42,) Tj
T*
(ensuring that class proportions remained stable across training and testing subsets. No) Tj
T*
(feature scaling was) Tj
T*
(applied because Random Forest is a tree-based algorithm and does not require) Tj
T*
(standardization for effective) Tj
T*
(performance.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(6.2 Model Selection and Hyperparameter Tuning) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(A Random Forest Classifier was selected because it handles non-linear relationships) Tj
T*
(well, is robust to noise,) Tj
T*
(and provides feature-importance estimates that make the results interpretable.) Tj
T*
