(NDVI emerged as the strongest predictor in this synthetic laboratory setting, followed) Tj
T*
(by GreenIndex and) Tj
T*
(Height. This suggests that vegetation health and morphology played the largest roles in) Tj
T*
(distinguishing crop-like) Tj
T*
(and weed-like samples in the generated dataset.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(9. Discussion) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The model delivered strong performance in a controlled laboratory demonstration. The) Tj
T*
(high precision indicates) Tj
T*
(that when the model predicts the presence of weeds, it is usually correct. The recall of) Tj
T*
(90.0% shows that most) Tj
T*
(weeds were detected, although a small number of weed samples were missed. In practical) Tj
T*
(agricultural use, missed) Tj
T*
(weeds could continue to compete with crops, so improving recall would be an important) Tj
T*
(direction for future work.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The experiment has several strengths. First, it uses interpretable features that are) Tj
T*
(meaningful in agricultural) Tj
T*
(monitoring, such as NDVI and GreenIndex. Second, the Random Forest algorithm handles) Tj
T*
(non-linear patterns well and) Tj
T*
(provides feature-importance estimates that make the model easier to explain. Third, the) Tj
T*
(use of cross-validation) Tj
T*
(and a held-out test set improves methodological reliability.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The main limitation is that the dataset is synthetic. Real field data would contain) Tj
T*
(lighting variation, soil) Tj
T*
(background changes, occlusion, weed diversity, and sensor noise that are not fully) Tj
T*
(represented here. Therefore,) Tj
T*
(the present results should be interpreted as a strong baseline laboratory study rather) Tj
T*
(than proof of field-ready) Tj
T*
(deployment.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(10. Conclusion) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
